A||||0||PaUL||My Fiero
Z||000000||PaUL||04-08-99||05:09 PM||||Hi all, seeing Ogre's background message made me want to post my own. I also like both computers and fieros. I work as a systems analyst. <p>Let me tell you about my car, an 86 SC. I bought this car in 88, soon after that the iron duke 4 cycl broke a rod or something because it was totally screwed. I replaced the 2.5 motor with a 2.8 v6 block I got out of a 85 celebrity and heads I found on an abandoned 80 citation. I got a lot of parts out of that old citation, the starter is still going strong till this day. I had the block bored and installed a new crank. The heads got a valve job and I tried to clean up the ports myself. A new cam and competition cams roller tip rockers were installed. <p>I completely gutted the computer system, cut all those sensor wires off at the bulkhead. I like computers but not in my car. I had to use the distributer out of the old citation, it still had vacuum and mech advance with no 4 terminal ECM connector. This HEI distributer has the coil screwed into the cap. I put in a replacement one from Accel.<p>For the intake, I used an Edlebrock manifold made for the F car and S10 truck. It fit with some minor filing. On top of this, Im using a Holley 4bbl carb with vacuum secondaries fed by a holley electric fuel pump. The stock TBI pump put out too much pressure, actually inflating the hose with gas. Im also still coping with the air cleaner getting clogged with dirt real fast. Need to figure something out to fix this.  <p>All the gauges were replaced with a custom made dashboard. Im using a speedo made by VDO, its electrically driven with the stock speed senser. The rest of the gauges are cheap ass units I got from JC whitney. <p>Well thats about it, I know this is a crude approach, and some of you might not like the idea of "mutilating" a fiero in this way, but it works for me. I like the idea of using 50's technology in an 80's car.     <p>
